//? Title: Forward ODE solvers: actual stages, attempts and trajectories
//? Subtitle: Six native methods, original decay convergence, oscillator drift and Lorenz portrait
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Derive the native one-step/adaptive/Adams methods from a tiny live-RHS example,
then retain the original six-method decay table and step-size sweep. Actual
callback/trial histories expose rejected adaptive steps, while the original
harmonic and Lorenz trajectories distinguish finite numerical diagnostics from
conservation or long-time accuracy claims.
)

\toc

> A finite forward initial-value problem

Solve y'=f(t,y),y(t_0)=y_0 for finite t_1>t_0 and a finite nonempty
fixed-dimensional state. Every actual RHS stage must return a finite derivative
of the same dimension. Native C++ can supply a live RHS subclass; named builtins
also expose exponential decay, logistic growth, harmonic oscillator, van der Pol
and Lorenz systems without requiring a callback subclass. This report makes no
blanket installation/director claim for all language bindings.

For smooth solutions in a stable fixed-step regime, Euler uses y_{n+1}=y_n+h
f(t_n,y_n), with local error O(h²) and accumulated global error O(h). Explicit
midpoint first forms k1=f(t,y),k2=f(t+h/2,y+hk1/2), then returns y+hk2, giving
second-order global accuracy. Classical RK4 uses

$$ k_1=f(t,y), \quad k_2=f(t+h/2,y+hk_1/2), \quad k_3=f(t+h/2,y+hk_2/2), \quad k_4=f(t+h,y+hk_3), $$
\alttext(The four classical stages evaluate the original state, two half-step states and one full-step state.)

$$ y_{next}=y+\frac h6(k_1+2k_2+2k_3+k_4). $$
\alttext(The classical RK4 update is the old state plus the step times a weighted average of the four actual derivative stages.)

RK4 has local error O(h^5) and fourth-order global accuracy under the stated
smoothness/stability assumptions. A final error-versus-step experiment estimates
global endpoint error, not local truncation error. None of these explicit
methods is universally stable on stiff problems.

> A tiny actual RK4 step from a live C++ RHS

For y'=−y,y(0)=1 and h=.5, actual stage derivatives are−1,−.75,−.8125,−.59375.
The proposal is.606770833333..., close to but different from exp(−.5). The
following complete editable program defines a genuine RHS subclass and reads its
actual callback records; it does not implement another RK4 recurrence in report
code.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ode_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
class ExponentialDecay : public dm::ode::RHS {
public:
    std::vector<double> evaluate(double, const std::vector<double> &state) override{return {-state[0]};}
};
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    ExponentialDecay rhs;
    dm::ode::ODESolver solver({dm::ode::StepMethod::RK4, 4, .5});
    auto t = solver.trace(rhs, {1.}, 0., .5);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.evaluations.size(); ++i) {
        auto &e = t.evaluations[i];
        d.node(std::to_string(i), {double(i % 2) * 9, 3. - double(i / 2) * 6},
               std::format("Actual stage {} at t={}\ny={} f={}", i + 1, e.time,
                           e.state[0], e.derivative[0]));
        if (i)
            d.edge(std::to_string(i - 1), std::to_string(i));
    }
    std::print("| actual stage | actual time | actual state | actual derivative |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.evaluations.size(); ++i) {
        auto &e = t.evaluations[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i + 1, e.time, e.state[0],
                   e.derivative[0]);
    }
    std::print("\nActual committed value: {:.15f}. Closed-form reference: {:.15f}. Actual stage calls: " "{}.\n",
               t.solution.y.back()[0], std::exp(-.5),
               t.solution.function_evaluations);
    d.save_svg("build/ode_tiny.svg");
}
```
